From 1690c6fedfa3cde6c00a95c38cb666a40a3a6b7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:06:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] test(lane-246 R60): paired S-X8/q8_0 mul_mm coverage across
 the small-n band, and the two refutations it produced

The brief commissioned two S-X8 prefill fixes. Both premises are refuted by receipts
taken in this tree; no switch is added and OPTIONS-REGISTRY gets no row.

Coverage added (eval + perf, same commit): the 4B prefill FFN shapes m=9216 k=2560
(ffn_gate/ffn_up) and m=2560 k=9216 (ffn_down) for SX8 and q8_0 at n=32/64/128/256/512.
R52b left the whole band between the mat-vec cutoff (n>8) and n=512 uncovered for S-X8
on any shape a model actually runs, and every prior S-X8-vs-q8_0 prefill pairing was
taken ACROSS TWO PROCESSES, which cannot separate a kernel effect from a run-level one.

Refutation 1 (deliverable 1, the small-n tile decode). Within one binary, one process,
6 interleaved rounds, the sx8/q8_0 ratio is FLAT in n, not decreasing:
m=9216 k=2560 reads 0.944/1.034/1.021/1.010/0.980 at n=32..512 - a tie bracketing zero;
m=2560 k=9216 reads 1.117/1.140/1.129/1.157 at n=32..256, non-overlapping but flat.
A decode cost that failed to amortise over few columns would shrink as n grows. It does
not, and per-tile decode work scales with n exactly as the MACs do, so it cannot. The
real S-X8 deficit is SHAPE-dependent (low m, high k), not width-dependent, so a smaller
tile for n<=128, a shared-memory decode reuse, or an MMVQ route table by n all attack a
cause that is not there.

Refutation 2 (deliverable 2, the elementwise dtype path). r60-evidence/10-dtypes.txt:
every non-S-X8 tensor carries the SAME type in both files - all 177 F32 norm/scale
tensors are F32 in both, all 152 shared q8_0 tensors are q8_0 in both, zero shape
mismatches. The only four differing classes are ffn_down/gate/up and token_embd, which
are the S-X8 tensors themselves. There is no f32-vs-f16 norm difference to convert, so
neither a loader-side conversion nor a fast f32 elementwise variant has a cause to fix.

What the elementwise 2x actually is: a run-level factor. In the cooled ABBA receipts the
byte-identical q8_0 attention matmuls are themselves 13-15% slower in the S-X8 process,
and some ops are FASTER there (SCALE 58.7 vs 66.9 us, MUL_MAT q8_0 m=32 55.4 vs 59.8 us)
- which no placement story survives. r60-evidence/31-alloc-placement-SUMMARY.txt closes
the placement door directly: both 4B files allocate into the identical memtype/heap
classes (14 allocs memtype 0 heap 0, 10 memtype 2 heap 2, 8 memtype 3 heap 1), zero bytes
in the slow uncached memtype 1, no spill. This confirms the read already written into
chain139-seat60-memtype.ps1:88-89, "a clock/state difference, not a kernel one". Settling
it needs loaded-model timing with clock telemetry in a quiet window, which is HQ ground.

No PPL cell is owed: no change here alters a byte of output.

Receipts: r60-evidence/ - 10-dtypes.txt, 20-mulmat-correctness.txt (2150/2150 pass, rc=0),
21-perf-smalln-r0..r5.txt (rc=0 each), 22-perf-smalln-TABLE.txt,
30-alloctrace-4b-{SX8,Q8}.txt (rc=0), 31-alloc-placement-SUMMARY.txt.

Origin: fork-authored (ArifiLabs lane-246 R60 S-X8 prefill)
Measured-effect: op-level perf-mode only (rows in receipts); served pp128 UNMEASURED in this lane - HQ pairs quiet
Lane: lane-246
Cherry-picked-from: 356c33aca (SOURCE PATHS ONLY, ArifiLabs lane-254 R66 integration)
Recut-onto: 1b6185951 (ArifiLabs lane-254 R66c, from 0d52b430d)
